  "query_preview": "..\n\nNext 7 Days...\n\nA hot, dry, and breezy weekend is in store for the entire region.\nToday will be the hottest day of the stretch where the entire CWA\nwill see highs in the upper 90s with localized triple digits. \nTemps will then return back to the low to mid 90s Sunday followed \nby a gradual cooldown through the work week. In addition to these \nwell above average temperatures this weekend, there will be very \nlittle moisture. Min RH values were in the teens across the CWA \nFriday and are only expected to get worse today. Winds have begun\nto shift out of the WNW ahead of a cold front which will help \nkeep the RH recoveries overnight poor followed by values \nbottoming out in the single digits for much of the CWA today. \n\nThe last piece of this fire weather puzzle is that of the winds.\nAs we rapidly increase the mixing height with hot temperatures\ntoday we will see 30 to 40 kt winds at the 700-500mb layer in \nplay to get mixed down to the surface. Even though the widespread \nwildfire smoke region-wide may keep temps down a pinch, these \nwinds are the strongest we have seen aloft in a while which is \ncause for concern. There are several \"what if\" questions for the \nfire weather ingredients but overall it looks to be a very clear \ncritical weather conditions day. As a result, a Red Flag Warning\nis in effect today from 11 AM to 9 PM MDT. For Sunday all of the \nsame ingredients are present, however all of them get slightly \nless favorable which will mean that the whole region will be in \nthe \"elevated\" level of fire weather concerns. \n\nWildfire smoke will remain an issue through the weekend and into\nearly next week. With the ridge continuing to dominate, the mid\nlevel flow will remain westerly and thus advecting the smoke from\nthe several PNW fires into our region. Additionally there are \nsome very smoky fires in SW MT increasing concentrations for \nlocations near Livingston where an AQA remains in effect at the \ntime of writing this. While Livingston will remain socked in smoke\nfor the next few days, the rest of the CWA will see surface smoke\ncome in and out due to shifting low level winds and subsidence even\nthough the smoke aloft will remain steady.\n\nLooking ahead to the work week there are still several questions\nregarding the forecast. What I can say with some confidence \nthrough is that a pattern change is in store for our area. As the\nIntermountain Ridge continues to battle with the Hudson Bay Low \nit appears that the ridge may back off just enough to allow waves \nof energy to pivot through the high plains. The first of these \ncould make an appearance on Monday, but models are still in \ndisagreement on just how far west the moisture and energy will \nmake it. As of now, the farthest west solution still keeps all of\nthe precip east of Miles City. The other good news with this \npattern change is that this would allow for the return of great \nplains moisture in the CWA, especially for SE Montana. This wind\nshift advecting in the low level moisture could also help in \ndecreasing some of the widespread smoke. After another shot at \nsomething weak midweek there is a continued signal for something \nof note to impact the entire area late week.\n\nAlthough the long range models still show some noteworthy\ndifferences between each other and from run to run, I am finally\nseeing at least some agreement. It appears that a pretty decent\nshortwave for this time of year could drop out of AB and into the\nplains bringing with it a chance for thunderstorms and a good\ntemperature cooldown behind a cold front. Ensembles are showing\nhigh temperatures Friday 5-10 degrees below average which will be\nquite welcome just a week after hitting triple digits yet again.\nThis feature will be the next thing to keep our eyes on as it\ncould be the first impactful precip event in several weeks.\nWMR/Archer",
